course: arts
lesson: 6
title: Charts, number ones and famous musicians as public record
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A music chart is a weekly list of the most popular songs, counted from real numbers: sales, streams and, in the US, radio plays; no judge picks number one.
explain: Public record means things anyone can check, like release dates, chart positions, sales estimates and awards; "the best band ever" is not on that list, because "best" is an opinion and only counts and dates are facts.
explain: Picture Grandad slamming the table about the Beatles: that they sold hundreds of millions of records is a fact you can check; that they were the best is his taste, and other people argue for other bands.
explain: Big numbers grow as they are passed around, rumours spread faster than proof, and songs often come from long before the film where you first heard them, so the wise move is to check before you repeat.
explain: A "fave" is a favourite, "end of" in British slang means no more argument, and "Shchedryk" is a Ukrainian New Year song whose tune the world now knows as "Carol of the Bells".
fact: The UK Official Charts count sales and, since 2014, streams; the US Billboard Hot 100 counts sales, streams and radio plays.
fact: The Beatles are one of the best-selling music acts in history, with sales estimated in the hundreds of millions; whether they were "the best" is an opinion.
fact: Michael Jackson's album Thriller, from 1982, is widely reported as the best-selling album of all time, usually estimated at around 70 million copies, not a billion.
fact: Taylor Swift's Eras Tour, from 2023 to 2024, was reported to be the highest-grossing concert tour ever, taking over 2 billion US dollars; "gross" means the total before costs, and crews, venues and travel are paid from it.
fact: Many people say modern K-pop began in 1992 with Seo Taiji and Boys; BTS became the first K-pop act to reach number one on the Billboard Hot 100, with "Dynamite" in 2020.
fact: Ukraine has won Eurovision three times: in 2004 with Ruslana, in 2016 with Jamala and in 2022 with Kalush Orchestra.
fact: Not only European countries take part in Eurovision: Australia has competed since 2015, and Israel takes part too.
fact: The Grammy Awards are voted on by members of the Recording Academy, people who work in music, not by fans.
fact: A rumour is a story passed around without proof; it is not a fact just because many people repeat it.
fact: Mariah Carey's "All I Want for Christmas Is You" came out in 1994 and first reached number one on the Billboard Hot 100 in December 2019, 25 years later.
fact: Number one means a song was bought, streamed or played most that week; it shows popularity, not quality, and many songs critics love never reached number one.
fact: "Carol of the Bells" comes from "Shchedryk", a Ukrainian New Year song arranged by the composer Mykola Leontovych; a Ukrainian choir sang it in the United States in the early 1920s, and Peter Wilhousky later wrote the English words.
exercise: Leo asks how they know which song is number one. What is the answer? | It is counted from sales, streams and plays | A judge listens to every new song and picks the best one | Radio DJs vote for their favourite song each week | The singer with the most fans online gets it
exercise: Since when have UK charts counted streams? | Since 2014 | Since 1954 | Since 1994 | They never have
exercise: Grandad says the Beatles were the best band ever, "and that's a fact". What is the fact part? | They are among the best-selling acts in history | Every music expert in the world agrees they were the best | No other band has ever sold a record | "Best" is something you can measure exactly
exercise: Kyle's uncle says Thriller sold "like a billion copies". What is widely reported? | Around 70 million copies | Exactly one billion copies | Fewer than a thousand copies | Ten billion copies, one for each person
exercise: Harper says the Eras Tour made more money than any tour. What is true? | It was reported as the top-grossing tour, but the singer keeps only part | All the ticket money went straight into Taylor Swift's own bank account | It earned less than a school concert | Nobody has any idea how much it took
exercise: The cousin in Toronto says BTS invented K-pop. What is true? | K-pop is older; many date modern K-pop to Seo Taiji and Boys in 1992 | BTS invented K-pop in 2020 with "Dynamite", and there was none before | K-pop was invented in Toronto by Canadians | K-pop does not exist outside of BTS
exercise: Which song made BTS the first K-pop act to top the Billboard Hot 100? | "Dynamite" | "Thriller" | "Shchedryk" | "Believe"
exercise: Mykola says Ukraine won Eurovision "loads of times". How many wins does Ukraine have? | Three: 2004, 2016 and 2022 | One, in 2022 only | Ten, more than any other country | None, Ukraine never entered
exercise: Max says only Europe can be in Eurovision. What is true? | Australia and Israel take part too | Only countries in the EU are allowed to enter | Only countries that speak English can enter | Only the country that won last year can enter
exercise: Bella will vote loads so her fave wins a Grammy. What is the problem? | Grammys are voted by music professionals, not fans | Fans can vote as many times as they like, so the biggest fan club wins | The singer chooses the Grammy winner herself | Grammys are decided by chart position alone
exercise: Jess saw on TikTok that a singer secretly hates her band. What is wise? | Treat it as a rumour until there is real proof | It must be true if everyone on TikTok is saying it | Share it with everyone to warn them | Believe it because TikTok checks everything
exercise: Ruth says the Christmas song was number one the year it came out. What happened? | It came out in 1994 and first hit US number one in 2019 | It was number one every single week of 1994 in every country | It has never been in any chart at all | It came out in 2019 and was number one at once
exercise: Abdul says, "If it's number one, it's the best song, end of." What is wrong? | Charts measure popularity, not quality | Nothing, the number one is always the best | Number one songs are always the very worst | Charts are picked by critics, so he is right
exercise: Mason says "Carol of the Bells" is from Home Alone. Where does the tune come from? | "Shchedryk", a Ukrainian New Year song | It was written specially for the film Home Alone | It is an old English hymn with no author | It was composed by Mariah Carey in 1994
exercise: Who arranged "Shchedryk"? | The Ukrainian composer Mykola Leontovych | The Hollywood director of Home Alone, in 1990 | The Beatles in Hamburg | Michael Jackson in 1982
exercise: What does "public record" mean? | Information anyone can check, like dates and chart places | Whatever most people are saying about something online | A song played in a public place | A record that is free to download
test: Your mate goes, "number one is just whatever the radio DJs fancy, innit." Is he right? | No, charts are counted from sales, streams and plays | yes, the DJs just pick their favourite every Sunday, mate | Yes, but only in Britain | No, the Queen used to pick it
test: What would happen to a song if lots of people streamed it every December for years? | It could climb the charts long after it came out | It would be banned from the charts | Its release date would change to that year | Nothing, old songs can never chart again
test: Grandad says, "The Beatles were the best, that's a fact." Gran says, "Rubbish, it was ABBA." Which is true? | "Best" is an opinion, so both are giving their taste | Grandad is stating a proven fact, because the Beatles sold more | Gran is stating a proven fact | Neither band ever sold any records
test: Your cousin says Taylor Swift "pocketed" all 2 billion dollars from the tour. What is wrong? | Gross is the total before crews, venues and travel are paid | Nothing, all of the money goes to the singer, every last dollar | The tour made no money at all | Ticket money goes only to the fans
test: A video says, "Eurovision: Europe only, no exceptions." Which country proves it wrong? | Australia, which has competed since 2015 | Ukraine, which won in 2022 | France, which is in Europe | Italy, which is in Europe
test: A kid in Seoul hears "BTS started K-pop" and sighs. Why? | K-pop grew for years before BTS became its biggest stars | BTS are not a Korean group | K-pop started only last year | BTS have never had a single hit in America or anywhere else
test: Bella says, "If enough of us vote, we'll win the Grammy for her." What is the truth? | Fans do not vote for Grammy winners | One vote per fan decides who wins the Grammy | The Grammy goes to the most-followed singer online | Grammys are given out by a lucky draw each year
test: Kateryna says the tune is Ukrainian. Mason says, "Prove it." What can she point to? | "Shchedryk", arranged by Leontovych and sung in the US in the early 1920s | Nothing, the tune was written in Hollywood for the film Home Alone | A TikTok video saying so | The fact that she likes the song
test: Uncle says Thriller sold a billion "because my mate told me". What is the wise move? | Check big numbers, because they grow when repeated | Believe it, because uncles are always right | Repeat it to everyone at school | Add a few more millions to be safe
test: Jess: "Everyone's saying the singer hates her band, so it's true." What is the flaw? | Many people repeating a rumour does not make it proof | Nothing, if everyone keeps saying it, then it has to be true | Rumours are always made up by the singer | Rumours are only false on weekends
test: Which of these is public record and not opinion? | The year a song reached number one | Which band was the coolest ever | Whose voice is the nicest | Which song is the best of all time
exam: Identify the principal basis upon which the Billboard Hot 100 is compiled. | The aggregation of sales, streams and radio plays | The deliberation of a panel of expert judges | The personal preference of radio presenters | A ballot of the readers of a newspaper
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the participation of states in the Eurovision Song Contest. | Certain states outside Europe, including Australia, are admitted as participants | Participation is confined exclusively to member states of the European Union | Participation is confined to states in which English is spoken | Participation is confined to the victor of the preceding contest
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attainment of first place in a weekly chart? | It denotes the greatest popularity during that week, not superior quality | It denotes a certification of quality by critics | It denotes that the composition is the best ever recorded | It denotes the personal approval of the performer
exam: Ascertain the origin of the melody widely known in English as "Carol of the Bells". | The Ukrainian New Year song "Shchedryk", arranged by Mykola Leontovych | An original composition for a motion picture of the 1990s | A traditional English hymn of unknown authorship | A composition by a performer of popular music in 1994
